If I was a less skilled statistician, I'd claim that I was due . . .

I took an afternoon to sit around and play--a rare thing, these days.

Working my way through the list of characters, I got as far as
tourist--not a good start when characters were dying that fast.

But he had a lot of cash, even for a tourist, making the protection
racket quite tempting.

My cat and I happily relieved two infidel shopkeepers withing the first
three levels of their cash--leaving me with *far* more than I needed 
(about 6700) . . . the loot also includeda pair of magic markers and an
expensive, though cursed, lamp.

Another room, another wand--when what to my wondering eyes should apear,
but a wishing wand, the first in two years.

Yes, I think it's been two years since I"ve found one before the castle.

Now, without the wand, I probably would have tried going wishless.  But
that's a lot easier with a wizard.

Happily, somehting in the back of my mind reminded me about the scroll
as the first wish--there was only one in the wand.

Now it's time to be *very* careful.  It took me forever to get poison
resistance, to the point I considered blessing the lamp and using it for
this.  Finally, my pets (I've been through about 5 cats, one dog, and
three horses) took out a unicorn.  The corpse did me no good, but the
horn solved my problems when I found a beehive.

I'm still not sure I did the right thing ing passing up the eyese of the
overworld, instead settling for speed boots, silver dragon mail, the eye
of taphioca, and that wonderful saber--but that's all that were to be
had.   The subsequent gift of giantslayer and discovery of excalibur in
a bones file make the use of future wishes unlikely (but then, what do I
need but a bag [grr--wrong sokoban!], a helmet, and gloves at this
point?  Four wishes could get the eyes, I suppose . . .)

Oh, and by the time I returned to minetown from the bottom, down to one
cat and a baby crocadile, the cat was a bit weak to deal with the guard
captain.  I sure wasn't going to give up my protection for his saber,
but then I got the crafty idea.  Hee-hee.  Putting him to sleep will
make *him* mad, but it's not murder.  Here, kitty-kitty . . .

